# Time-Series Forecasting Splits (Leakage Prevention)

When building solar forecasting models (PVDAQ + NSRDB), follow this checklist before training and evaluation.

## 1) Define the forecasting setup up front
- Specify the prediction target (PV output), forecast horizon(s), and the time granularity (e.g., 15-min, hourly).
- Clearly define the time index column and timezone; keep timezone handling consistent end-to-end.
- Record the data coverage window (start/end timestamps) and any filtering rules applied.

## 2) Use time-aware validation (no shuffling)
- Use expanding window or rolling window splits for time-series forecasting.
- Ensure each validation/test period happens strictly after the corresponding training period.
- Do not shuffle rows; if you resample, resample consistently before splitting.

## 3) Prevent leakage in feature engineering
- Treat the training portion as the only source of information for any “fit”/aggregation step.
- Never compute rolling statistics, normalization parameters, imputations, or joins using future (validation/test) timestamps.
- When joining PVDAQ and NSRDB features:
  - align timestamps first (same timezone and resolution),
  - then validate overlap coverage for train vs validation/test,
  - and record how many rows are dropped due to missing alignment.

## 4) Evaluation protocol
- Evaluate with MAE, RMSE, and R2, reported consistently for each model and split.
- Include residual checks:
  - residuals vs time-of-day/season,
  - residual magnitude across clear vs cloudy regimes when you can identify them.

## 5) Reproducibility notes (important for portfolio notebooks)
- Log split boundaries (train/val/test start/end timestamps) in the notebook markdown.
- Keep split logic in a small reusable helper function so results are repeatable.

